+Observing and hearing are joined by an "or", as though either would do. But looking is a property of the crossing. The sightline is there whenever anyone is, and it does not have to be sent. Hearing, at Staniforth Road, is a message with a sender: someone reaching for a control, choosing between two devices, deciding what length is adequate. Its ordinary state is silence. And on every previous circuit those two runners had made — fewer than ten of them, and on none of them had they met a tram at the crossing — the silence had been true. A channel that is almost always silent, and almost always silent truthfully, teaches the people who use it that silence is information.
 